WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.446
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.446 --> 00:00:06.920
This video shows our TF-Gripper, rollouts of our RETAF model, and comparisons with the baselines.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.795
Our gripper uses force control and is driven by a timing belt.

00:00:11.795 --> 00:00:13.960
The two fingers can be driven separately.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.853
Our gripper has very low control latency.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.740
This helps when an AI model controls it in real time.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.491
Our gripper can be mounted on robot arms of different models.

00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:34.260
In this video we only change the adaptor to fit it on a KUKA robot.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.987
We use our device to teleoperate the robot and control the force.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:42.740
With a very small force, it picks up a potato chip and places it in the plate.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:53.656
Here we compare our RETAF model with a pure diffusion policy using force and using width.

00:00:53.656 --> 00:00:56.250
RETAF succeeds, while the baselines crush the chip.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.500
On the liquid transfer task, RETAF transfers the liquid successfully, while the baseline fails.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.180
When grasping tofu, RETAF uses tactile sensing to judge the force more accurately, and picks the tofu up successfully.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.260
We train on four kinds of potato chips and evaluate on two different kinds.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.660
Diffusion policy with force control does not work well in distribution, and it does not work well out of distribution either.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:30.904
But RETAF works well both in distribution and out of distribution.

00:01:30.904 --> 00:01:32.930
This shows its strong generalization.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.039
We put our TF-Gripper and the trained RETAF model directly on an SO-ARM robot arm.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:51.580
We teleoperate the robot to pick up a potato chip and let RETAF control the force, and it also succeeds.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.990
This shows the generalization of both our gripper and our model.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.525
TF-Gripper and RETAF also work on contact-rich tasks.

00:01:59.525 --> 00:02:03.070
They perform better than a diffusion policy that controls force.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.353
TF-Gripper is not only for force-regulated manipulation.

00:02:11.353 --> 00:02:13.839
It can also be used for many everyday tasks.

00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:16.550
This shows its value for the robotics community.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:27.057
In summary, TF-Gripper enables robot learning for force-regulated manipulation, and works across diverse tasks.

00:02:27.057 --> 00:02:34.614
RETAF outperforms the baselines on force-regulated tasks, generalizes to unseen objects, and transfers to a new robot with the same TF-Gripper.

00:02:34.614 --> 00:02:35.830
Thank you for watching.
